    <div class="prose">
      <p>Everyone wants a media wall. The recessed electric fire, the TV set into the wall, the panelling and downlights: it's the feature every living room on social media has, and homeowners are actively searching for someone to build one. The demand is there. The problem is getting in front of it before the next installer does.</p>

      <h2>Why media walls are made for Facebook ads</h2>
      <p>This is the easiest trade in the world to sell on video, because the transformation is dramatic and instant. A blank chimney breast becomes a showpiece wall, and a homeowner scrolling their feed cannot look away. That's exactly what a good ad needs. We use <strong>real footage of real builds</strong>, filmed on site or cut from work you've already done, never stock, because a media wall sells itself the second someone sees it done well.</p>

      <div class="split">
        <div class="prose" style="margin:0;">
          <h2>What the results look like</h2>
          <p>Media walls respond fast to ads because the demand is already there. In one recent campaign, around <strong>£500 of ad spend brought in 36 qualified leads</strong>, homeowners who put their details in and told us exactly what they wanted building.</p>
          <div class="callout">
            <p>That's the point of the system: a modest budget turned into a diary full of real enquiries, filtered so you're only talking to people who actually want the job done.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div>
          <figure style="max-width:300px;margin:0 auto;border:1px solid var(--border);border-radius:14px;overflow:hidden;background:#14110c;">
            <video style="width:100%;display:block;" controls muted playsinline preload="none" poster="/clips/PMW_Showcase.jpg" aria-label="Media wall installation ad footage">
              <source src="/clips/PMW_Showcase.mp4" type="video/mp4">
